# Regression test for a deadlock that used to happen when capturing a job's result as a
# value (`$var := $(...)`, a named argument like `join`'s, etc.):
# src/lang/value/value_definition.rs's ValueDefinition::JobDefinition::eval started the
# job on a worker thread, then called `threads().join_one(id)` -- which blocks until
# that thread *fully exits* -- before ever reading the value the thread produced. That's
# fine for a job that sends one small value, but a stream's own row channel (`streams()`
# in src/lang/pipe.rs) only buffers 128 rows. Past that, the producer thread blocked on
# `output.send()` waiting for a reader, and the only candidate reader was stuck waiting
# for the producer to exit -- deadlock. src/lang/command/closure.rs's per-statement eval
# had the identical shape, so a closure body producing more than 128 rows was affected
# the same way. Fixed by only waiting for the *value*, not the producing thread(s), when
# a job's result is being captured -- see GlobalState::recv_job_result's doc comment.
#
# This never showed up before because everything in the existing test suite streams far
# fewer than 128 rows at a time. It was found via `language_reference.md`'s own `join`
# example, which joined `files` (usually well under 128 entries) against `users:list`
# (which enumerates every system account -- 300+ on a real machine).
#
# Run the same shape twice, with different (but equally oversized) data each time, so a
# fix that only happens to work on the first occurrence doesn't slip through. Auto
# discovered like any other tests/*.crush golden test; a regression here is caught by
# run_system_test's own 30s per-test timeout (tests/system.rs) rather than hanging the
# whole suite.

$left1 := $(seq 1 1001 | rename value=id)
$right1 := $(seq 1 1001 | rename value=id)
$joined1 := $(join id=$left1 id=$right1 | count)
assert ($joined1 == 1000) "joining two 1000-row streams should produce 1000 matching rows"

$left2 := $(seq 500 1500 | rename value=id)
$right2 := $(seq 500 1500 | rename value=id)
$joined2 := $(join id=$left2 id=$right2 | count)
assert ($joined2 == 1000) "a second, independent 1000-row join should also complete and produce the correct row count"

echo "ok"
